b/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060472 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060472 #edb/ref 1061 10429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060472 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "860a330d-51b9-5aba-a7ce-25da9f5b8254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060472 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060474 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060474 #edb/ref 1061 10432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060474 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec4f5b67-5c3e-5ce2-bb3a-7b6b95c2a0c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060474 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060475 #edb/ref 1061 10434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060475 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab1f256a-5f6e-5d12-9b0f-482675761ab1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060475 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060770 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060770 #edb/ref 1061 40878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060770 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "53a0851d-e1e9-5f13-a1f9-1e0ad720c677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060770 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060771 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060771 #edb/ref 1061 40879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060771 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6325c0ca-8510-5c9d-8b87-b9042fe377ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060771 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060772 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060772 #edb/ref 1061 40882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060772 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "149280d9-7b92-52c0-ab69-a011aca574b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060772 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060773 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060773 #edb/ref 1061 40883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060773 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9d9e1551-d8ec-536f-9036-9555d43eae22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060773 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060774 #edb/ref 1061 40887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060774 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6f9b14a3-07a8-5f6b-9c69-4df4a979ee4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060774 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060775 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060775 #edb/ref 1061 40993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060775 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "082973dc-66d1-5d9c-9dc3-ae132f451431" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060775 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060782 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060782 #edb/ref 1061 41408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060782 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b373d760-0d5d-5ec6-a830-9c562d00e5be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060782 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060874 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider an equilateral triangle with sides of length $2x$. Notice that any altitude will split it into two congruent triangles, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/301fe26a314d91d0d316758a5076cea2383f325991789addceaec71f0e7b16dd.png)\n\nIn the new smaller triangle the acute angles will be $30^\\circ$ and $60^\\circ.$ Such triangles are called **$\\boldsymbol{30^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{60^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{90^\\circ}$ triangles**.\n\nBased on the picture above, we see a special property shared by all $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles:\n\n*In any ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the hypotenuse is twice as long as the shortest leg (which is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle).*\n\nThis also works in the other direction:\n\n*If the hypotenuse is twice as long as the shortest leg, then the given right triangle is a ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060874 #edb/ref 1065 2383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060874 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e07c8310-fe8a-5f3e-9ff0-7cd4e93a784c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060874 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060875 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the same ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle from before. This time, however, we will focus on the height ($h$) instead of the hypotenuse.\n\n![](images/30/301fe26a314d91d0d316758a5076cea2383f325991789addceaec71f0e7b16dd.png)\n\nApplying the Pythagorean Theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{h}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{2} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\sqrt{3}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the second important property:\n\n*In any ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg (which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle) is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the length of the shorter leg (which is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle).*\n\nWe can summarize everything we know about ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangles using the diagram below:\n\n![](images/85/8598f9163b29833bb5895416e3dec9b6aa2358bd2cfcca944625ebb9f576b3a4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060875 #edb/ref 1065 2386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060875 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e2dbb6c4-dedc-56fa-8e66-87e843a7f7fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060875 #edb/ref 1067 "The Longer Leg" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060969 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the region below, which represents the inequality $2 < x < 5.$\n\n![](images/fe/feeae0bfa9b29e6f034a3fd33d1e9fbd369840835af2d6588892ee6edc2fa120.png)\n\nWe can represent this inequality using **interval notation** as follows:\n\n$$\n(2,5)\n$$\n\nThe parentheses $(\\phantom{0})$ tell us that the endpoints $x=2$ and $x=5$ are *not* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval does not include the endpoints, we say that it is **open**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (2,5).\n$$\n\nThe symbol $\\in$ means \"lies in\" or \"belongs to.\" So, the above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (open) interval $(2,5).$*\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060969 #edb/ref 1065 6750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060969 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dbd2ff75-3253-5df6-ac32-55d5e3a95cf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060969 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060970 #edb/ref 1064 "Some intervals may include one parenthesis and one bracket. \n\nFor example, consider the following interval:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{\\big[}}} 1,4 {\\color{red}{\\big)}}\n$$\n\nTo express this interval as an inequality, we note the following:\n\n- The left bracket $\\,\\color{blue}[\\,$ tells us that left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq}}$ for this endpoint.\n\n- The right parenthesis $\\,\\color{red})\\,$ tells us that the right endpoint $4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{<      }}\\,$ for this endpoint.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq  }}\\,\\,   x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,\\, 4.\n$$\n\nThe interval can be graphed as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a67872e8193c2bcc249a9a9006acb916909099e4babdbe5cdfa6d2643c10ad2.png)\n\nIntervals with one bracket and one parenthesis are called **half-open** (or **half-closed**)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060970 #edb/ref 1065 6751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060970 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "633202af-a5bd-58ac-a806-89458305a7a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060970 #edb/ref 1067 "Half-Open Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060973 #edb/ref 1064 "Now consider the region below, which represents the inequality $-3 \\leq x \\leq 1.$\n\n![](images/38/38c5bf5408bbbeb0f63e198e0564a37e8fe6a34d7f2ce340751d8000443123ed.png)\n\nWe express this inequality using interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-3,1]\n$$\n\nThe brackets $[\\phantom{0}]$ tell us that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=1$ *are* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval includes the endpoints, we say that it is **closed**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1].\n$$\n\nThe above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (closed) interval $[-3,1].$*\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060973 #edb/ref 1065 6821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060973 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "27f305da-f09b-53e5-a8b8-a30d29eb0f15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060973 #edb/ref 1067 "Closed Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064360 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064360 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying a Fraction of Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064360 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186063947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064360 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "68cd4840-bb77-5065-884a-ce1c8c309cd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064361 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064361 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064361 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064361 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064361 #edb/ref 1039 "Dividing a Counting Number by a Fraction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064361 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186063948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064361 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1ca49b4e-a4fa-5a67-9334-12e8656b5d1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064362 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064362 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064362 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064362 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064362 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064362 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064362 #edb/ref 1039 "Dividing a Fraction by a Counting Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064362 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186063949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064362 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "56936b9b-2b52-58a7-ae3f-7d51492b8247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064415 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064415 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064415 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064415 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064415 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064415 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064415 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064415 #edb/ref 1045 "Fractions of Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064415 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064415 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064415 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064415 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "eb9087bf-30e8-5a71-8438-1b9d7e3953ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065287 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065287 #edb/ref 1061 79637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065287 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "000dfe16-b4b5-5050-b07d-e01d48c6261b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065287 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065288 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065288 #edb/ref 1061 79638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065288 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d17d6b7d-6849-50e4-a34b-78c672570998" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065288 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065289 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065289 #edb/ref 1061 79642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065289 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e39488bb-4bd7-5dfe-b572-7901ceb4d1c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065289 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065290 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065290 #edb/ref 1061 79643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065290 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "450d83cb-4e1d-5817-81f1-d89746298ceb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065290 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065291 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065291 #edb/ref 1061 79647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065291 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05b3f520-be5d-5978-b74c-77dab033ac85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065291 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065292 #edb/ref 1061 79648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065292 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8697e561-0d74-5f5b-821a-7c762a1d34a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065292 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065421 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's think about how to simplify the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe expression above is a fraction, but there are additional fractions in the numerator and denominator.\nSuch expressions are called **fractions of fractions**.\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nThe trick to working with fractions of fractions is to remember that a fraction represents a division. So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)} = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) \\boldsymbol{\\div} \\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{\\color{red}{4}}{\\color{blue}{3}}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{2}{3})÷(\\frac{3}{4}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{2}{3})\\times (\\frac{4}{3}) & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\times 4}{3\\times 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{9} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065421 #edb/ref 1065 8182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065421 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5da17ec4-93cc-5398-bbfc-d50405cd2fad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065421 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065422 #edb/ref 1064 "How do we simplify $\\dfrac{5}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}?$ Notice that, this time, we have a counting number divided by a fraction.\n\nThe trick is to write $5 = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{5}}{\\color{red}{1}}$ and perform the same process as before:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{(\\frac{2}{3})} & = \\\\ 5÷(\\frac{2}{3}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{1})÷(\\frac{2}{3}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{1})\\times (\\frac{3}{2}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\times 3}{1\\times 2} & = \\\\ \\frac{15}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065422 #edb/ref 1065 8183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065422 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "80cee0a8-050d-53ca-8a81-4b6e37f71cdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065422 #edb/ref 1067 "Dividing a Counting Number by a Fraction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065423 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to simplify a fraction of fractions like $\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)}{4}.$ In this case, we have a fraction divided by a counting number. We have two methods available.\n\nThe long way is to write $4 = \\dfrac 4 1$ and then calculate in the same way as we did earlier:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{5}{3})}{4} & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{3})÷4 & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{3})÷(\\frac{4}{1}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{3})\\times (\\frac{1}{4}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\times 1}{3\\times 4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{12} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA quicker way is to recognize that the result will have the same numerator as the top fraction and that the denominator is just the product of the two denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{5}{3})}{4}=\\frac{5}{3\\times 4}=\\frac{5}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065423 #edb/ref 1065 8185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065423 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "03013ab2-d0a1-57fa-817f-9dd124de63f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065423 #edb/ref 1067 "Dividing a Fraction by a Counting Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068068 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068068 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068068 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068068 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068068 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068068 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068068 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068068 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186503170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068068 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1f1a378b-e807-52c5-ac81-76d5297f2e2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Line Given a Point on the Line and Its Slope" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bc85ca17-8a08-5473-9473-dce954132bd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
